{-
   Which storage `let &x = ...` names.

   A mutable binding used to allocate a slot and write the initializer into it, always. Where the
   initializer *built* its value, that is a second allocation and a whole-value copy for storage
   nothing else had a name for - so the binding takes the temporary over instead, which is what an
   immutable binding has always done.

   The elision is only sound because of the second function below, and that is the whole reason this
   fixture is a pair rather than a shape assertion. Aliasing an existing local is harmless for a name
   that cannot be written and a miscompile for one that can, and the difference is invisible in the
   IR: `%b = alloc` followed by a copy and `%b` naming `%a` print as different programs but only one
   of them is wrong, and only when something writes through the name afterwards.
-}

data Cell {v: Int}

-- The initializer built its value here and nothing else names it, so the binding is that storage.
fn adopted() -> Int:
    let &c = Cell {v: 1}
    c.v = 41
    return c.v

-- The initializer names storage the program already had, so the binding needs its own: writing
-- through `b` may not reach `a`. 1 + 99 rather than 99 + 99.
fn copied() -> Int:
    let a = Cell {v: 1}
    let &b = a
    b.v = 99
    return a.v + b.v

-- And the same one level in: the value is a field of a fresh temporary rather than the whole of it,
-- which is storage the record still owns and not a temporary to be taken over.
fn field() -> Int:
    let &inner = Cell {v: 7}.v
    inner = inner + 1
    return inner

fn main() -> Int = adopted() + copied() + field()
